stages.notes:
1. Lint gating per step: _lint_gate today gates only the item's last implement step (earlier steps report, without auto-fix, because a later step may use an import). Prompt assumes every step gates; with an Improve child per step that is the consistent choice. Owner decides.
2. required_trivial_reviews 0 (brief rule) needs quality.check_loop_packet to stop requiring a trivial report on a complete packet when the contract's value is 0; otherwise use 1.
3. Exit 2 is not re-checked by script today; adding a suite-check rerun at implement's done makes the exit condition script-backed.
4. Per-step Improve focus (new constant IMPLEMENT_STEP_REVIEW_FOCUS, IMPROVE_* style):
Implementation step review focus. This result is one step of the item's step plan, and its tests are written
later from the test spec, so this review is the only check of the code before them. Review the step's diff (the
step's paths since the step began) and fix, within those paths, any of these:
- the change does not do the step's task, or misses a criterion as the spec's R-<n> states it, or does work no
  criterion asks for (a later step's work, a feature the item does not request);
- a file changed outside the step's paths, or unrelated work reverted or reformatted;
- a new or changed public entry point that does not check its arguments before any effect, swallows an error or
  returns a success default (Code craft 2);
- a public function without its contract docstring, a comment that restates code, or dead code (rules 3-4);
- code placed outside architecture.md's ## Structure or ## Conventions, or a departure with no new AD-<n> under
  ## Decisions; a name that collides or is exported wider than needed (rule 8);
- a near-copy of existing logic, a new unit where an existing one could be reused, composed or augmented, or a
  dependency, option or fallback with no present need (rules 1, 9);
- a failure that is not diagnosable, or user-facing text assembled from fragments or bypassing the catalog
  (rules 6-7); for a UI step, a plain or static interaction where the plan calls for a rich one (rule 5);
- a step skill listed in the packet and not used, or used against its stated purpose, with no reason in the result;
- a behaviour the code now has that no test-spec case covers and the result does not list for the test stage;
- a recorded check command that fails, or a new lint finding on a changed line with no waiver, after your last edit.
Do not write tests, start a later step, or edit a recorded command. Rerun the step's check commands and the printed
lint command after your last edit and record their exit codes. Commit your edits. A pass that finds none of these is
trivial.

<CODE_CRAFT, verbatim>
5. COMMON's 'Only planning results ... get an actual Improve-skill handoff' must name implement steps.
6. CODE_CRAFT placement: keep the single constant; render it in full to <run>/reference.md#code-craft and quality/code-craft.md; point implement and test FILES at it; embed it verbatim in IMPLEMENT_STEP_REVIEW_FOCUS, verify's review loop and END_REVIEW_FOCUS (fresh contexts read no other file reliably). Never a condensed copy.
7. The ask-agent parallel-chain paragraph must survive (reference.md#parallel-chain) while delegation ask-agent exists (owner decision 10 does not remove it).
